{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law cap and return","requirement":"A landlord may not take a security deposit above one and one-half times one month's rent, may raise it by at most 10% a year, and must return it with interest, less itemized deductions, within 30 days after the tenancy ends.","key_value":"one and one-half times one month's rent; yearly increase max 10%","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey where the landlord chooses to collect a deposit; account rules differ for landlords of 10 or fewer units and 10 or more. Seasonal use of 125 days or fewer is excluded.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Account rules turn on unit count (10 or fewer units vs 10 or more); seasonal rentals of up to 125 days excluded"},"exemptions":"Seasonal use or rental of not more than 125 consecutive days by a person having a permanent place of residence elsewhere.","penalty":"Tenant may sue for double the amount of the security deposit wrongfully withheld, plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-19 et seq.","quoted_span":"The maximum-security deposit to be collected by the landlord cannot be more than one and one-half times one month’s rent ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2 ). It can be less. Any additional yearly security deposit increase may not exceed 10% of the current security deposit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Safe Housing Act security deposit return after domestic-violence lease termination","requirement":"When a tenant ends a lease under the Safe Housing Act because of domestic violence, the landlord must make the deposit and interest available within 15 business days and notify the tenant in writing within 3 business days where and when to collect it.","key_value":"15 business days","coverage_conditions":"Leases terminated early by a tenant who is a victim of domestic violence under the Safe Housing Act; transient or seasonal rentals are not covered.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Applies to residential leases terminated by domestic violence victims"},"exemptions":"Transient or seasonal rentals.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6","quoted_span":"Within 15 business days after the lease is terminated, the landlord shall make available the return of the tenant’s security deposit, plus any interest earned, to the tenant or the tenant’s agent.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
